.TH RINGBUFF 3 "October 2026" "ringbuff" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
ringbuff_init, ringbuff_free, ringbuff_push, ringbuff_pop, ringbuff_peek,
ringbuff_is_empty, ringbuff_is_full, ringbuff_size \- fixed\-capacity FIFO ring buffer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqringbuff.h\(dq
.PP
.B typedef struct ring_buffer_t *ring_buffer_t;
.PP
.BI "ring_buffer_t ringbuff_init(size_t " capacity ", size_t " element_size );
.BI "void ringbuff_free(ring_buffer_t " buff );
.PP
.BI "int ringbuff_push(void *" x ", ring_buffer_t " buff );
.BI "void ringbuff_pop(void *" out ", ring_buffer_t " buff );
.BI "void *ringbuff_peek(ring_buffer_t " buff );
.PP
.BI "bool ringbuff_is_empty(ring_buffer_t " buff );
.BI "bool ringbuff_is_full(ring_buffer_t " buff );
.BI "size_t ringbuff_size(ring_buffer_t " buff );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions implement a first\-in, first\-out queue of fixed\-size elements
stored in a circular buffer.
The capacity and element size are chosen when the buffer is created and do not
change.
Elements are copied into the buffer on insertion and copied out on removal, so
the caller always keeps ownership of its own objects.
The library never returns memory that the caller must free.
.PP
The type
.I ring_buffer_t
is an opaque handle (a pointer to an incomplete structure).
Callers must not dereference it.
.PP
The functions are not thread\-safe.
Callers sharing a buffer between threads must provide their own locking.
.SS ringbuff_init()
Allocates a buffer able to hold
.I capacity
elements of
.I element_size
bytes each.
The buffer starts empty.
.SS ringbuff_free()
Releases the buffer and its storage.
The handle must not be used afterwards.
Pointers previously returned by
.BR ringbuff_peek ()
become invalid.
.SS ringbuff_push()
Copies
.I element_size
bytes from
.I x
to the tail of the queue.
Existing elements are never overwritten: if the buffer is full, the call fails
and the contents are unchanged.
.SS ringbuff_pop()
Removes the element at the head of the queue (the oldest one) and copies it to
.IR out .
.I out
must point to storage of at least
.I element_size
bytes.
Once the last element is removed, the buffer returns to its initial empty
state and can be refilled to full capacity.
.PP
If the buffer is empty, nothing is copied, the buffer is unchanged, and
.I out
is not modified.
Because the function returns no value, it cannot report this case; callers
should test
.BR ringbuff_is_empty ()
or
.BR ringbuff_size ()
first.
.SS ringbuff_peek()
Returns a pointer to the element at the head of the queue, which is the element
the next
.BR ringbuff_pop ()
would remove, without removing it.
The pointer refers to the buffer's internal storage.
It must not be freed, and it should be treated as invalid after any subsequent
call to
.BR ringbuff_pop ()
or
.BR ringbuff_free (),
since the slot may be reused.
.SS ringbuff_is_empty(), ringbuff_is_full(), ringbuff_size()
Report whether the buffer holds no elements, whether it holds
.I capacity
elements, and the current number of stored elements, respectively.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR ringbuff_init ()
returns a new handle on success, or
.B NULL
if memory could not be allocated.
.PP
.BR ringbuff_push ()
returns 0 on success and \-1 if the buffer is full.
.PP
.BR ringbuff_peek ()
returns a pointer to the oldest element, or
.B NULL
if the buffer is empty.
.PP
.BR ringbuff_is_empty ()
and
.BR ringbuff_is_full ()
return true or false.
.BR ringbuff_size ()
returns the number of elements currently stored, from 0 to
.IR capacity .
.PP
.BR ringbuff_pop ()
and
.BR ringbuff_free ()
return no value.
.SH ERRORS
The functions do not set
.IR errno
themselves.
.BR ringbuff_init ()
may fail with the errors of
.BR malloc (3).
.SH NOTES
None of the functions check their arguments for
.BR NULL .
Passing a null or already freed handle, a null
.I x
to
.BR ringbuff_push (),
or a null
.I out
to
.BR ringbuff_pop ()
on a non\-empty buffer results in undefined behavior.
.PP
.BR ringbuff_init ()
does not validate its arguments.
A
.I capacity
or
.I element_size
of zero, or a product that overflows
.IR size_t ,
leads to undefined behavior or an undersized allocation.
Callers should validate these values.
.PP
.BR ringbuff_push ()
reads, and
.BR ringbuff_pop ()
writes,
.I element_size
bytes through their pointer argument, so that pointer must refer to an object
at least that large.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include "ringbuff.h"
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    ring_buffer_t q = ringbuff_init(4, sizeof(int));
    if (q == NULL)
        return 1;

    for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
        if (ringbuff_push(&i, q) == \-1)
            printf("full, dropped %d\en", i);
    }

    int *front = ringbuff_peek(q);
    if (front != NULL)
        printf("next: %d\en", *front);

    while (!ringbuff_is_empty(q)) {
        int v;
        ringbuff_pop(&v, q);
        printf("%d\en", v);
    }

    ringbuff_free(q);
    return 0;
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR memcpy (3)
